# Django and Code Style

## Language

Use English for:
- Python class names
- function names
- variables
- model field names
- URLs and internal identifiers when practical

Use Spanish for:
- user-facing labels
- validation messages
- verbose_name
- template text
- interface copy

## Django conventions

Prefer explicit and readable Django code.

Do not over-engineer.

Use:
- ModelForm for model-backed forms
- inlineformset_factory for parent/detail forms when appropriate
- login_required for authenticated views
- PermissionDenied when an authenticated user lacks required permissions
- transaction.atomic for multi-step operations that must succeed or fail together

## Queries

Use select_related or prefetch_related when the template accesses related objects repeatedly.

Avoid obvious N+1 query patterns.

## Forms

Do not expose system-managed fields unnecessarily.

Examples of fields that generally should not be manually editable during WorkOrder creation:
- status
- assigned_technician
- scheduled_date
- installation_started_at
- completed_at
- created_by
- timestamps

created_by should normally come from request.user.

## Permissions

Do not confuse permission scope with object scope.

Example:
- Comercial may have view_workorder.
- The queryset must still restrict them to WorkOrder.objects.filter(commercial=request.user).

Do not give broad change_workorder permission to Jefe Técnica merely to allow scheduling.
Use the custom schedule_workorder permission.

## Templates

All application pages should extend the shared base template when appropriate.

Maintain the current SGOT dark design language.

Primary colors:
- background: #0B0E14
- surface: #141821
- elevated surface: #1B202C
- border: #252B38
- primary: #6C5CE7
- primary hover: #7D6FF0
- secondary: #22D3EE
- primary text: #F1F3F8
- muted text: #9AA3B5

Avoid:
- excessive gradients
- exaggerated shadows
- generic landing-page styling
- inline CSS unless unavoidable

SGOT should look like professional internal business software.